  "query_preview": "..\nAn upper-level trough associated with a surface low has begun its\ndeparture eastward and will continue to support precipitation \ntoday. Most of our region may only see rain, however, a transition\nto mixed precipitation to snow is possible at lower elevations. \nThis is contingent upon the timing of dropping below the freezing \nmark while the low to our east continues to produce precipitation.\nWhile this is a possible scenario, accumulations in lower \nelevations have a high chance (75%) of only seeing trace amounts. \nHigher elevations like the Little Rockies and portions of NW \nPhillips County have the best shot at seeing accumulating snow \nbetween 1-2 inches of snow. Timing for precipitation cessation has\nbeen trending between tonight and the early AM hours on Thursday.\n\nWith temperatures dipping into the low 30s/upper 20s tonight, the\nsoil temperature will be warmer than the environment, which has a\nlow chance (25%) of leading to patchy and/or dense fog. This also\ncould exist due to subsidence as clear skies are forecast to \nenter as the low exits the region. Expect gusty conditions through\nthe day today as the wind gradient is tightening. \n\nLarge scale upper troughing continues across the\nwestern US the remainder of the week. The next system will bring \nanother shot of arctic air from the north this weekend. Some \nlight snow is possible but the main impact will be the below \nnormal temps. Wind chills this weekend are forecast to be in the \nteens during the AM hours and potentially dipping in the single \ndigits. Any additional accumulations tomorrow onward (for areas \nlower in elevation) are anticipated to see trace amounts, if that.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Snowfall forecast totals have been trending similar to previous\n  update. Moderate to high confidence in snowfall totals (60%-80%).\n\n- Confidence in temperatures is moderate (40%-60%).\n\n- Confidence in fog developing this evening is low (10%-20%).\n\n- Confidence in timing of precip cessation is high (80%).\n\nFINDLEY",
